dict(pas="5.1", w="子/the Master | 謂/said of | 公冶長：「=gōng yě cháng/Gongye Chang — a disciple//Gong- (公冶, a two-character surname; 'duke')//-ye (of the surname; 'to smelt')//Chang (his personal name) | ^可/(he) can | 妻=qì/be given a wife | 也。/(assertion) | ^雖/though | 在/(he) was in | 縲絏=léi xiè/bonds — prison//black rope (for binding prisoners)//to tie, fetters | 之/'s | 中，/midst | ^非/(it was) not | 其/his | 罪/crime | 也。」/(assertion) | ^以/(he) took | 其/his (own) | 子/child — here a daughter | 妻=qì/(and) gave (her) as wife | 之。/to him",
  l="The Master said of Gongye Chang: “He can be given a wife.\nThough he was in bonds,\nit was not his crime.”\nHe took his own daughter and gave her to him as wife.",
  i="The Master said of Gongye Chang: “He is a man one could marry a daughter to. He has been in prison, but the crime was not his.” And he married his own daughter to him.",
  n="The book is named 公冶長 from its first words, the name of a disciple. Zhu Xi: every passage in it weighs men of past and present, worthy or not, right or wrong in what they did — one strand of 'investigating things and exhausting principle'; it has 27 passages, and he records Hu Yin's view that much of it was perhaps written down by Zigong's followers. 公冶長 Gongye Chang (surname Gongye) was a disciple; He Yan's Kong Anguo says he was a man of Lu. 謂 with an object is 'to speak of, say of'. 妻 is read qì here and in the last line, the verb 'to give (a man) a wife' (Zhu Xi: 妻去聲…為之妻也), not qī 'wife'. 縲絏 léi xiè are the black ropes that bound prisoners (Zhu Xi: 縲黑索也，絏攣也), so 'in bonds' is 'in prison'. 以其子妻之 'taking his child, he wived him with her': 以 'taking', 子 'child', here a daughter, 之 'him'. Zhu Xi: nothing can be found out about what kind of man Gongye Chang was, but since the Master called him fit to marry he must have had something to recommend him; and since his time in bonds was not for his own crime, it did him no harm — guilt or innocence lies in oneself, and how can honour or disgrace come from outside?"),
dict(w="子/the Master | 謂/said of | 南容：「=nán róng/Nan Rong — a disciple//Nan (from Nangong, his house)//Rong (from his courtesy name Zirong) | ^邦/(when) the state | 有/has | 道，=dào/the Way | 不/(he is) not | 廢；=fèi/left unused | ^邦/(when) the state | 無/lacks | 道，=dào/the Way | ^免=miǎn/(he) escapes | 於/from | 刑=xíng/punishment | 戮。」=lù/(and) execution | ^以/(he) took | 其/his | 兄/elder brother | 之/'s | 子/child — a daughter | 妻=qì/(and) gave (her) as wife | 之。/to him",
  l="The Master said of Nan Rong: “When the state has the Way, he is not left unused;\nwhen the state lacks the Way,\nhe escapes punishment and execution.”\nHe took his elder brother's daughter and gave her to him as wife.",
  i="The Master said of Nan Rong: “When the state is well governed, he will not be passed over; when it is badly governed, he will escape the headsman.” And he gave him his elder brother's daughter in marriage.",
  n="南容 Nan Rong, Zhu Xi says, was a disciple who lived at Nangong 南宮 (居南宮, usually taken as his surname), personal name Tao 縚 (also given as Kuo 适), courtesy name Zirong 子容, posthumous name Jingshu 敬叔, and elder brother of Meng Yizi of 2.5 — though both the identification with Nangong Jingshu and which brother was the elder are disputed; He Yan's Wang Su calls him Nangong Tao, a man of Lu. 不廢 'not left unused' — Zhu Xi: he would surely be employed; because he was careful in word and deed, he could serve at a well-ruled court and escape harm in a disordered age. He comes back in Book 11. 刑 is punishment, 戮 putting to death. 妻 is qì again, 'to give as wife'. Zhu Xi records a view that Gongye Chang was the lesser man, so the Master gave him his own daughter and Nan Rong his brother's, favouring his brother over himself; he quotes Master Cheng against it: this is peering at the sage with a selfish heart — whoever avoids suspicion is lacking within; the sage is perfectly impartial, and in marrying off a girl one weighs the man to find her match; the ages of the couples and the order of events cannot be known, and to call it avoiding suspicion is quite wrong — a worthy man would not do that, still less a sage."),
