dict(pas="5.2", w="子/the Master | 謂/said of | 子賤：「=zǐ jiàn/Zijian — the disciple Fu Buqi//Master//Jian (from his courtesy name) | ^君子/a gentleman | 哉/(exclamation) | 若/this | 人！/man | ^魯/(if) Lu | 無/had no | 君子/gentlemen | 者，/(supposing that) | ^斯/this (man) | 焉=yān/where | 取/(would he) have taken | 斯？」/this",
  l="The Master said of Zijian: “A gentleman indeed, this man!\nIf Lu had no gentlemen,\nwhere would this one have taken this from?”",
  i="The Master said of Zijian: “There is a gentleman for you! Yet if Lu had no gentlemen in it, where could he have found the means to become one?”",
  n="子賤 Zijian is Fu Buqi 宓不齊 (宓 is read fú), a disciple; He Yan's Kong Anguo writes the surname 密. 若人 'this man' (He Yan's Bao Xian: 若此人也). 者 at the end of a clause can mark it as a supposition: 魯無君子者 'supposing Lu had no gentlemen'. 焉 is yān, 'where, from what?' (Zhu Xi: 於虔反). The two 斯 are not the same: Zhu Xi takes the first as 'this man' and the second as 'this virtue'. Zhu Xi: Zijian was one who could honour worthy men and choose his friends so as to perfect his own virtue; the Master first sighs at his worth and then asks where he could have got it if Lu had no gentlemen — which shows, too, how many worthies Lu had. He quotes Su (蘇氏): in praising a man's goodness one always traces it to his father and elder brothers, his teachers and friends — the height of generosity."),
