dict(pas="5.3", w="子貢/Zigong | 問/asked | 曰：「/saying | 賜/(I,) Ci | 也/(pause) | 何如？」=hé rú/what of (me) | ^子/the Master | 曰：「/said | 女，=rǔ/you | 器/a vessel | 也。」/(assertion) | ^曰：「/(Zigong) said | 何/what | 器/vessel | 也？」/(question) | ^曰：「/(the Master) said | 瑚璉=hú liǎn/a hu-lian — a jade-decked grain vessel of the ancestral temple//hu (the Xia name for it)//lian (the Shang name for it) | 也。」/(assertion)",
  l="Zigong asked, saying: “And Ci — what of him?”\nThe Master said: “You — a vessel.”\nHe said: “What vessel?”\nHe said: “A hu-lian.”",
  i="Zigong asked: “And what do you make of me?” The Master said: “You are a vessel.” “What kind of vessel?” “One of the jade-trimmed vessels that hold the grain at the temple offerings.”",
  n="賜 Ci is Zigong's personal name (1.15); speaking of himself by it is the humble form. 女 is rǔ, 'you' (Zhu Xi: 女音汝). 器 is a vessel, a finished thing made for a use (Zhu Xi: 有用之成材) — the 器 of 2.12 'the gentleman is not a vessel', which hangs over the answer. 瑚璉 hú liǎn (Zhu Xi: 璉力展反): vessels that held the millet offered in the ancestral temple — called 瑚 under the Xia, 璉 under the Shang, and 簠簋 under the Zhou — and decked with jade, the most precious and beautiful of vessels. He Yan's Bao Xian gives the same names and calls them the most precious of temple vessels; the Liji ('Mingtang wei') has the two names the other way round, 璉 for the Xia and 瑚 for the Yin. Zhu Xi: Zigong saw that the Master had granted Zijian the name of gentleman, so he asked about himself; the answer means that though he had not reached 'not being a vessel', he was at least a precious one. Variant: He Yan's text reads 賜也如何, the same question in other order."),
