dict(pas="5.4", w="或/someone | 曰：「/said | 雍/Yong — Ran Yong, Zhonggong | 也/(pause) | 仁/(is) humane | 而/but | 不/not | 佞。」=nìng/glib",
  l="Someone said: “Yong is humane but not glib.”",
  i="Someone said: “Yong is humane, but he has no gift for talk.”",
  n="雍 Yong is Ran Yong 冉雍, courtesy name Zhonggong 仲弓, a disciple (Zhu Xi; He Yan's Ma Rong). 也 after the name sets him up as the topic. 佞 nìng is a ready tongue (Zhu Xi: 口才也) — in the Analects nearly always a fault. 而 here is 'but'. Zhu Xi: Zhonggong was weighty, plain and sparing of words, and the people of the time took a ready tongue for worth, so they praised him for his virtue and regretted that he lacked the talent."),
dict(w="子/the Master | 曰：「/said | ^焉=yān/what | 用/use (is there for) | 佞？=nìng/glibness | ^禦=yù/meeting | 人/others | 以/with | 口=kǒu/(the) mouth's | 給，=jǐ/readiness | ^屢=lǚ/often | 憎=zēng/(one is) hated | 於/by | 人。/others | ^不/(I do) not | 知=zhī/know | 其/his | 仁，/humaneness | ^焉=yān/what | 用/use (is there for) | 佞？」=nìng/glibness",
  l="The Master said: “What use is glibness?\nMeeting others with a ready mouth,\none is often hated by others.\nI do not know about his humaneness —\nbut what use is glibness?”",
  i="The Master said: “What does he want with a clever tongue? Those who fend people off with quick answers mostly earn their dislike. Whether he is humane I cannot say; but what would he want with a clever tongue?”",
  n="焉 is yān, 'how, what' (Zhu Xi: 於虔反): 焉用佞 'what use glibness?'. 禦 is 'to meet, to answer' (Zhu Xi: 當也，猶應答也); 給 jǐ is 'ready, quick to supply' (Zhu Xi: 辦也); 憎 is 'to hate' (Zhu Xi: 惡也). 於 after a verb can mark the doer: 憎於人 'hated by others'. Zhu Xi: the glib man answers others only with readiness of mouth and no real feeling, and merely gets himself hated; 'though I do not know whether Zhonggong is humane, his not being glib is just what makes him worthy, and no fault' — and saying 'what use glibness?' twice drives it home. Why will the Master not grant him humaneness? Zhu Xi: the Way of humaneness is so great that only one whole in it and unceasing can bear the name; even Yan Hui, next to a sage, could not keep from lapsing after three months (Book 6), so the sage could not lightly grant it to Zhonggong. He Yan's Kong Anguo: 屢 'often'; the glib man's words are quick, and he is often hated by the people. Variant: He Yan's text reads 禦人以給，屢憎民 'answering others with readiness, often hated by the people', and adds 也 after each 焉用佞 and after 不知其仁."),
