dict(pas="5.5", w="子/the Master | 使/would have | 漆雕開=qī diāo kāi/Qidiao Kai — a disciple//Qi- (漆雕, a two-character surname; 'lacquer')//-diao (of the surname; 'to carve')//Kai (his personal name) | 仕。=shì/take office | ^對/(he) answered | 曰：「/saying | 吾/I | 斯/this | 之/(marks the fronted object) | 未/not yet | 能/can | 信。」=xìn/be sure of | ^子/the Master | 説。=yuè/was pleased (= 悅 yuè)",
  l="The Master would have Qidiao Kai take office.\nHe answered, saying: “This — I cannot yet be sure of it.”\nThe Master was pleased.",
  i="The Master wanted Qidiao Kai to take up office. He replied: “Of this I am not yet confident.” The Master was pleased.",
  n="漆雕開 Qidiao Kai was a disciple, courtesy name Ziruo 子若 (Zhu Xi); He Yan's Kong Anguo: Qidiao the surname, Kai the name. 使…仕 'to have him take office'. 吾斯之未能信 puts the object first: 斯 'this' stands before the verb, marked by 之, the pattern of 2.6 唯其疾之憂. 説 is the edition's form of 說, read yuè, 'pleased' (Zhu Xi: 説音悦). Zhu Xi: 斯 points to the principle of the thing — what one must know to govern others; 信 is truly knowing it to be so without a hair's breadth of doubt; Kai said he could not yet, so could not yet govern men, and the Master was pleased at how earnest his will was. He quotes Master Cheng: Kai had already seen the main idea, so the Master was pleased; and Xie Liangzuo: nothing is known of Kai's learning, but if the sage sent him into office his talent must have fitted him for it; in the subtle workings of the heart, a single hair not yet his own meant he was not yet sure — this the sage could not know and Kai knew for himself; his talent was enough for office, but his measure would not rest in small attainment. He Yan's Zheng Xuan: the Master was glad that his will was so deeply set on the Way."),
